Reviewed independently, focusing on correctness beyond what Codex/CodeRabbit already flagged (Codex's point about exposing the pointer tracking as a declarative Stimulus action instead of manual `addEventListener`/`removeEventListener` is reasonable style feedback and I won't repeat it).

**Stale `_pressTarget` can outlive the gesture that set it** (`app/components/DS/dialog_controller.js:31-34` and `:70-75`).

`_pressTarget` is only cleared inside `clickOutside()`:

```js
this._onPress = (e) => { this._pressTarget = e.target; };
...
clickOutside(e) {
  const pressTarget = this._pressTarget;
  this._pressTarget = null;
  if (shouldCloseOnClick(pressTarget, e.target, this.contentTarget)) { ... }
}
```

If a `pointerdown` fires inside the content but the matching `click` never reaches this element (e.g. the user starts a text-selection drag inside the dialog and releases the mouse button outside the browser window/viewport, or the gesture is cancelled some other way), `_pressTarget` is left pointing at an inside node with nothing to reset it. The controller doesn't `disconnect()`/`connect()` again between opens for a dialog that isn't shown with `showModal()` — the code explicitly supports this: `if (this.element.matches(":modal")) return;` skips the focus trap for non-modal dialogs, implying some `DS::Dialog` instances stay open/closed/reused without a fresh Stimulus lifecycle. For such a dialog, a later, unrelated interaction that produces a `click` on this element without its own preceding `pointerdown` — most plausibly a keyboard activation (Enter/Space) of some outside, still-focusable control, since non-modal dialogs aren't focus-trapped here — would read the stale "inside" press target and incorrectly refuse to close.

This is a narrow, hard-to-hit edge case (needs an interrupted inside-press followed by a press-less outside click on the same dialog instance), so I'm not confident it's worth blocking on, but it's a real gap in the fix's model: the press target is "latched" rather than tied to the gesture it came from. A more robust version would also clear it on `pointerup`/`pointercancel` (or track it per `pointerId`) rather than relying solely on the next `clickOutside` call to reset state left by a different interaction.

Nothing else stood out — the core fix (distinguishing where the press started from where the click landed) is sound and the new helper's test cases cover the intended cases well.

